fbcon: re-arm the cursor blink when the console lock is busy

fb_flashcursor() gives up when console_trylock() fails and does not queue
itself again. From then on the cursor stands still until the next write to
that console re-arms the work through fbcon_cursor(). On a machine that is
driven over ssh, whose serial console is a legacy 8250 holding console_lock
for the length of every printk, and whose drivers print a few lines
whenever the picture is handed back to fbcon, that next write may never
come: the HY310 came back to a console with a frozen cursor after every
handback (doku/nachtlog/S12 section B, measured 08.09.2026 with the frame
buffer hash standing still until "\e[?25h" was written to the tty).

Queue the work again for the next blink interval instead. The lock is
still not waited for, so the unbind case the FIXME is about is unchanged.

--- a/drivers/video/fbdev/core/fbcon.c	2026-09-08 01:50:21.520200298 +0200
+++ b/drivers/video/fbdev/core/fbcon.c	2026-09-08 01:50:21.526101226 +0200
@@ -382,8 +382,19 @@
 	/* instead we just fail to flash the cursor if we can't get
 	 * the lock instead of blocking fbcon deinit */
 	ret = console_trylock();
-	if (ret == 0)
+	if (ret == 0) {
+		/*
+		 * Try again next blink. Returning without re-arming leaves the
+		 * cursor standing until something is written to the console --
+		 * on a box driven over ssh, with a slow serial console that holds
+		 * console_lock for the length of every printk, that can be
+		 * forever: measured on the HY310 after every picture handback
+		 * (doku/nachtlog/S12 B).
+		 */
+		queue_delayed_work(system_power_efficient_wq, &par->cursor_work,
+				   par->cur_blink_jiffies);
 		return;
+	}
 
 	/* protected by console_lock */
 	info = par->info;
